{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 5769 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "436bfab300565ccb7718c132475a60927060d8ac0ea2d5ffb5b8e777cd7e62f9" :edb/db-before-t 5768 :edb/db-after-t 5769 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539081 50 #inst "2026-10-23T17:58:33.536Z" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100705 1071 "Calculate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100705 1071 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100721 1071 "If $$\n\\displaystyle z_1=2\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) \\right]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle z_2=4\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\right],\n$$ find the quotient $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100721 1071 "If $z_1=2\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) \\right]$ and $z_2=4\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\right]$, find the quotient $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100722 1071 "Calculate the quotient $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle z_1=6\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) \\right]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle z_2=4\\left[\\cos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right].\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100722 1071 "Calculate the quotient $\\dfrac{z_1}{z_2}$ given that $z_1=6\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) \\right]$ and $z_2=4\\left[\\cos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right]$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100723 1071 "If $$\n\\displaystyle z_1=2\\left[\\cos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle z_2=6\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\right],\n$$ find the the product $z_1z_2$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100723 1071 "If $z_1=2\\left[\\cos\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right]$ and $z_2=6\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\right]$, find the the product $z_1z_2$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100724 1071 "If $$\n\\displaystyle z_1=2\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle z_2=4\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right],\n$$ calculate the product $z_1z_2$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100724 1071 "If $z_1=2\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right]$ and $z_2=4\\left[\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) +\\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right]$, calculate the product $z_1z_2$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100744 1071 "Calculate the range of validity of the binomial expansion\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{128}{(4+x)^3} = 2 - \\dfrac{3}{2}x + \\dfrac{3}{4}x^2 - \\cdots.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100744 1071 "Calculate the range of validity of the binomial expansion\n$\\dfrac{128}{(4+x)^3} = 2 - \\dfrac{3}{2}x + \\dfrac{3}{4}x^2 - \\cdots$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100747 1071 "The complex square roots of the number $$\n\\displaystyle z = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$ are given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle z_k^{1/2} = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{a}}+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}\\cdot{k}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{a}}+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}\\cdot{k}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $k=0,\\,1$. What are the values of $a$ and $b$?" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100747 1071 "The complex square roots of the number $z = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}\\right)$ are given by\n$z_k^{1/2} = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{a}}+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}\\cdot{k}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{a}}+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}\\cdot{k}\\right)$,\n\nwhere $k=0,\\,1$. What are the values of $a$ and $b$?" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100748 1071 "The complex square roots of the number $$\n\\displaystyle z = 9\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right]\n$$ are given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle z_k^{1/2} = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{a} \\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}} + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c}\\cdot{k}\\right) + \\textrm{i} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}} + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c}\\cdot{k}\\right) \\right],\n$$\n\nwhere $k = 0, \\, 1$. What are the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$?" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100748 1071 "The complex square roots of the number $z = 9\\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right]$ are given by\n$z_k^{1/2} = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{a} \\left[\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}} + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c}\\cdot{k}\\right) + \\textrm{i} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{b}} + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{c}\\cdot{k}\\right) \\right]$,\n\nwhere $k = 0, \\, 1$. What are the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$?" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100758 1071 "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}g(x)\n$$ for the function given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5}}{3x-7}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100758 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}g(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5}}{3x-7}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100758 1074 "To compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} g(x),\n$$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the right-sided limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1^+}g(x)$ does not exist since $g(x)$ is not defined in the interval $x\\in(1,2).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}g(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100758 1074 "To compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} g(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the right-sided limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1^+}g(x)$ does not exist since $g(x)$ is not defined in the interval $x\\in(1,2).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}g(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100759 1074 "Let's make a substitution\n\n$$\nz=e^y\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^y\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$ Consequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of  $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}+z=e^x\\,,\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(1)=e^{y(1)}=e^1=e\\,.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side of the differential equation is now an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(1)=e\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1}+C}{1} & =e \\\\ e+C & =e \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz  =   \\frac{e^x}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =z \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$ So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100759 1074 "Let's make a substitution\n\n$$\nz=e^y\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^y\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$ Consequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of  $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}+z=e^x\\,,\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(1)=e^{y(1)}=e^1=e\\,.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side of the differential equation is now an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(1)=e\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1}+C}{1} & =e \\\\ e+C & =e \\\\ C & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz  =   \\frac{e^x}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =z \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$ So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100772 1071 "Suppose that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{n = 1}^\\infty {\\dfrac{{{{(-1)}^n}}}{{\\sqrt[4]{{{n^5}}}}}}\n$$\nand that $s_2$ is the second partial sum of $S$. According to the alternating series error bound, $\\mid S-s_2 \\mid < k$ for some real number $k$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100772 1071 "Suppose that\n$S = \\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{n = 1}^\\infty {\\dfrac{{{{(-1)}^n}}}{{\\sqrt[4]{{{n^5}}}}}}$\nand that $s_2$ is the second partial sum of $S$. According to the alternating series error bound, $\\mid S-s_2 \\mid < k$ for some real number $k$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100774 1071 "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac {(-1)^n}{n}\n$$\nand that $s_i$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S$, use the alternating series error bound to determine the values of $i$ such that $\\mid S - s_{i} \\mid < 0.001$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100774 1071 "Given that\n$S = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac {(-1)^n}{n}$\nand that $s_i$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S$, use the alternating series error bound to determine the values of $i$ such that $\\mid S - s_{i} \\mid < 0.001$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100774 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\na_n =\\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac {(-1)^n}{n}\n$$\n\nThen $a_n$ is decreasing for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand $a_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$. So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}S - s_i & < & b_{i + 1}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}b_{i + 1} &= & ((-1)^{i + 1})/(i + 1)\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\le 0.001 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{i + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}\\le 0.001 \\\\ \\frac{1}{i + 1}\\end{vmatrix}\\le \\frac{1}{1000} \\\\\n&= i + 1\\begin{vmatrix}\\ge 1000 \\\\ i\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 999\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100774 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\na_n =\\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac {(-1)^n}{n}\n$$\n\nThen $a_n$ is decreasing for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand $a_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty$. So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n$\\begin{vmatrix}S - s_i & < & b_{i + 1}\\end{vmatrix}$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}b_{i + 1} &= & ((-1)^{i + 1})/(i + 1)\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\le 0.001 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{i + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}\\le 0.001 \\\\ \\frac{1}{i + 1}\\end{vmatrix}\\le \\frac{1}{1000} \\\\\n&= i + 1\\begin{vmatrix}\\ge 1000 \\\\ i\\end{vmatrix}\\ge 999\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100775 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\ln(2n+1)}{n}\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4^n}{n!}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100775 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\ln(2n+1)}{n}$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4^n}{n!}$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100775 1074 "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let\n$a_n= \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}$.\nThen, we have $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n=\\infty,\n$$ since the exponential term in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}\n$$\nis divergent.\n- For the second series, we use the comparison test. Observe that\n$\\ln (2n + 1) > 1⟹(\\ln (2n + 1))/(n) > \\frac{1}{n}$\nfor all $n\\geq 1$. Now, the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, by the comparison test, the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\ln(2n+1)}{n}\n$$\nis also divergent.\n- For the final series, we use the ratio test. Let\n$a_n= \\dfrac{4^n}{n!}$.\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle L|= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid; = lim_(n → ∞)((4^{n + 1})/((n + 1)!) \\div \\frac{4^{n}}{n!}); = lim_(n → ∞)((4^{n + 1})/((n + 1)!) \\cdot \\frac{n!}{4^{n}}); = lim_(n → ∞)((4(4^{n}))/(n!(n + 1)) \\cdot \\frac{n!}{4^{n}}); = lim_(n → ∞)((4(4^{n}))/(n!(n + 1)) \\cdot \\frac{n!}{4^{n}}); = lim_(n → ∞)\\left(\\frac{4}{n + 1}\\right); = 0.\n$$\nSince $L=0 < 1$, we conclude that the series is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series III converges." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100775 1074 "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let\n$a_n= \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}$.\nThen, we have $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n=\\infty$, since the exponential term in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{n^{99}}$\nis divergent.\n- For the second series, we use the comparison test. Observe that\n$\\ln (2n + 1) > 1⟹(\\ln (2n + 1))/(n) > \\frac{1}{n}$\nfor all $n\\geq 1$. Now, the series\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n}$\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, by the comparison test, the series\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\ln(2n+1)}{n}$\nis also divergent.\n- For the final series, we use the ratio test. Let\n$a_n= \\dfrac{4^n}{n!}$.\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get\n$L|= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid; = lim_(n → ∞)((4^{n + 1})/((n + 1)!) \\div \\frac{4^{n}}{n!}); = lim_(n → ∞)((4^{n + 1})/((n + 1)!) \\cdot \\frac{n!}{4^{n}}); = lim_(n → ∞)((4(4^{n}))/(n!(n + 1)) \\cdot \\frac{n!}{4^{n}}); = lim_(n → ∞)((4(4^{n}))/(n!(n + 1)) \\cdot \\frac{n!}{4^{n}}); = lim_(n → ∞)\\left(\\frac{4}{n + 1}\\right); = 0$.\nSince $L=0 < 1$, we conclude that the series is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series III converges." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100776 1071 "If the series $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent and $a_n$ is a positive, strictly decreasing sequence for all $n\\geq 1$, then which of the following statements *must* be true?\n\n1. Given that $f(n) = a_n$, where $f(x)$ is continuous and strictly decreasing, then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$ converges.\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (-1)^n a_n\n$$ is convergent.\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right| = 1.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100776 1071 "If the series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent and $a_n$ is a positive, strictly decreasing sequence for all $n\\geq 1$, then which of the following statements *must* be true?\n\n1. Given that $f(n) = a_n$, where $f(x)$ is continuous and strictly decreasing, then $\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ converges.\n2. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (-1)^n a_n$ is convergent.\n3. $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right| = 1$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100776 1074 "Let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The sequence $a_n$ and the function $f(x)$ satisfy the conditions for the integral test. According to the integral test, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$ is convergent.\n- Statement II is true. Since $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent, we must have that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n = 0.\n$$ By the alternating series test, then, $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (-1)^n a_n\n$$ is convergent.\n- Statement III is false. Recall that with the ratio test, $$\n\\displaystyle L = \\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right| = 1\n$$ gives no conclusion as to whether the series converges or diverges. In other words, there exist convergent series with $L=1$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II must be true." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100776 1074 "Let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The sequence $a_n$ and the function $f(x)$ satisfy the conditions for the integral test. According to the integral test, since $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent, then $\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ is convergent.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent, we must have that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n = 0$. By the alternating series test, then, $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (-1)^n a_n$ is convergent.\n- Statement III is false. Recall that with the ratio test, $L = \\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left| \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\\right| = 1$ gives no conclusion as to whether the series converges or diverges. In other words, there exist convergent series with $L=1$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II must be true." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186100786 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=5f\\left(\\dfrac12x\\right),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = 2\n$$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right).$\n\n![](images/49/49614f79caf13a140495c49e8a77e69aac506d03baa23849ea3806118b9046d2.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right).$\n\n![](images/d4/d46eb3aa45d473518b0a33cfcb652e2fa6e7018afb81db0d60e85f38b6e6766e.png)" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186100786 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n