nd the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ that satisfies $y(1)=e.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186101539 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\left(\\frac{1+x}{x}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =\\frac{K{e}^{x}}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{K{e}^{1}}{1+1} & =e \\\\ K & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}\\,.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186101539 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\left(\\frac{1+x}{x}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =\\frac{K{e}^{x}}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =e \\\\ \\frac{K{e}^{1}}{1+1} & =e \\\\ K & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{x+1}\\,.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186101545 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$ that satisfies $y(1)=2.$ Assume that $x>0.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186101545 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies $y(1)=2.$ Assume that $x>0.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186101545 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $x>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+C} & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =2 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{-1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186101545 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $x>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+C} & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =2 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\