nswer this question." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186101931 1064 "Suppose we want to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)\n$$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n$$\nf(x) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{x}^{3} \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ for } \\hspace{1em} x < 1 \\\\ 2 \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ for } \\hspace{1em} x = 1 \\\\ - ( x - 1 {)}^{3} + 3 \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ for } \\hspace{1em} x > 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),\n$$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186101931 1064 "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n$$\nf(x) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{x}^{3} \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ for } \\hspace{1em} x < 1 \\\\ 2 \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ for } \\hspace{1em} x = 1 \\\\ - ( x - 1 {)}^{3} + 3 \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ for } \\hspace{1em} x > 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186101932 1064 "Suppose we want to find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)\n$$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),\n$$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186101932 1064 "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186101965 1064 "We've been using the fact that $AI_n = I_n A = A$ for any $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2.$ That is, let's prove that $AI_2=I_2A = A,$ where $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}\\cdot 1+{a}_{12}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{11}\\cdot 0+{a}_{12}\\cdot 1 \\\\ {a}_{21}\\cdot 1+{a}_{22}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{21}\\cdot 0+{a}_{22}\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186101965 1064 "We've been using the fact that $AI_n = I_n A = A$ for any $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2.$ That is, let's prove that $AI_2=I_2A = A,$ where $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}\\cdot 1+{a}_{12}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{11}\\cdot 0+{a}_{12}\\cdot 1 \\\\ {a}_{21}\\cdot 1+{a}_{22}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{21}\\cdot 0+{a}_{22}\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186102002 1064 "Earlier, we determined that the remainder $R_5$ for the series\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^2}\n$$\n\nshould satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{6}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_5 \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{5}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, first recall that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}, \\qquad x\\geq 1\n$$\n\nsatisfies the conditions for the integral test.\n\nLet's visualize $f(x)$ and the corresponding terms\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nusing a diagram.\n\n![](images/e0/e05dfba3ddf77605991d30a440fb40bd21f264329264369f3ba6ca283842bc34.png)\n\nWe can represent the sum of our series using the areas of vertical bars with a width of $1$ in the following two ways:\n\n![](images/ac/acff3370099b31f0675fdb31c8670c7ff09e26c0af23f3ad41705db59b8f27b4.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- On the left diagram, the *top-left* corner of the $n$th bar coincides with $a_n$.\n- On the right diagram, the *top-right* corner of the $n$th bar coincides with $a_n$.\n\nIn both cases, the partial sum $s_5$ is represented by the combined area of the first $5$ bars, and the remainder $R_5$ is represented by the combined area of the remaining bars.\n\nFinally, consider the following two diagrams.\n\n![](images/a4/a403f48a51f50a57f698ae9e86dbdfdcb1c239f96f1f8f58872a3c3bf24cb2b8.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- On the left diagram, the bars corresponding to $R_5$ contain the area below the curve for $x\\geq 6$. This means that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_6^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\leq R_5.\n$$\n- On the right diagram, the area below the curve contains the bars corresponding to $R_5$ for $x\\geq 5$. This means that\n$$\n\\displaystyle R_5 \\leq \\displaystyle \\int_5^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nCombining these two inequalities, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\int_{6}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x \\leq R_5 \\leq \\int_{5}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186102002 1064 "Earlier, we determined that the remainder $R_5$ for the series\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^2}\n$$\n\nshould satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{6}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_5 \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{5}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, first recall that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}, \\qquad x\\geq 1\n$$\n\nsatisfies the conditions for the integral test.\n\nLet's visualize $f(x)$ and the corresponding terms\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nusing a diagram.\n\n![](images/e0/e05dfba3ddf77605991d30a440fb40bd21f264329264369f3ba6ca283842bc34.png)\n\nWe can represent the sum of our series using the areas of vertical bars with a width of $1$ in the following two ways:\n\n![](images/ac/acff3370099b31f0675fdb31c8670c7ff09e26c0af23f3ad41705db59b8f27b4.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- On the left diagram, the *top-left* corner of the $n$th bar coincides with $a_n$.\n- On the right diagram, the *top-right* corner of the $n$th bar coincides with $a_n$.\n\nIn both cases, the partial sum $s_5$ is represented by the combined area of the first $5$ bars, and the remainder $R_5$ is represented by the combined area of the remaining bars.\n\nFinally, consider the following two diagrams.\n\n![](images/a4/a403f48a51f50a57f698ae9e86dbdfdcb1c239f96f1f8f58872a3c3bf24cb2b8.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- On the left diagram, the bars corresponding to $R_5$ contain the area below the curve for $x\\geq 6$. This means that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_6^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\leq R_5$.\n- On the right diagram, the area below the curve contains the bars corresponding to $R_5$ for $x\\geq 5$. This means that\n$R_5 \\leq \\displaystyle \\int_5^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x$.\n\nCombining these two inequalities, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\int_{6}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x \\leq R_5 \\leq \\int_{5}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186102018 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,2]$ as shown below. How can we calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x?\n$$\n\n![](images/1e/1e98d2090150d84908fd17b7e86fffef5bb3c085f52e26272cda579c15126cce.png)\n\nFirst, we notice that the graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [-2,0]$ and the line segment for $x \\in [0,2].$ Therefore, using the additive property of the definite integral, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis can be represented using definite integrals. We divide the region between our curve and the $x$-axis as follows:\n\n![](images/3e/3eabe4f363f93b7e4d005c6c1f0beba8e9ff9e74ebae5410930565baa8569e41.png)\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186102018 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,2]$ as shown below. How can we calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x?$\n\n![](images/1e/1e98d2090150d84908fd17b7e86fffef5bb3c085f52e26272cda579c15126cce.png)\n\nFirst, we notice that the graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [-2,0]$ and the line segment for $x \\in [0,2].$ Therefore, using the additive property of the definite integral, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis can be represented using definite integrals. We divide the region between our curve and the $x$-axis as follows:\n\n![](images/3e/3eabe4f363f93b7e4d005c6c1f0beba8e9ff9e74ebae5410930565baa8569e41.png)\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186102027 1064 "Suppose that we have the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 (x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$ Can we solve it using the method of integration by substitution?\n\nThe answer is yes, and the procedure is very similar to the one that we followed for the indefinite integrals. The main difference is that this time, we need to change the limits of integration too. We proceed via three steps, as follows.\n\n** Step 1**: Change of variable in the integrand. \n\nWe start by setting the base of the power equal to $u.$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x=u^5\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\n** Step 2**: Calculate the new limits. \n\nWe now have to change the limits of integration, $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Since we want to express the integral in terms of the new variable, $u,$ we need to change the variable in the limits too.\n\nThe lower limit $x=1$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =1-1 \\\\ u & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile the upper limit $x=2$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =(2)-1 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is useful to use a table like the following when changing limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Solve the integral in the new variable $u,$ using the new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{6}-\\frac{1}{6}(0)^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when applying integration by substitution to definite integrals, there is no need to substitute the variable $x$ back in the final answer. We can simply evaluate it using the new variable $u.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186102027 1064 "Suppose that we have the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 (x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Can we solve it using the method of integration by substitution?\n\nThe answer is yes, and the procedure is very similar to the one that we followed for the indefinite integrals. The main difference is that this time, we need to change the limits of integration too. We proceed via three steps, as follows.\n\n** Step 1**: Change of variable in the integrand. \n\nWe start by setting the base of the power equal to $u.$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x=u^5\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\n** Step 2**: Calculate the new limits. \n\nWe now have to change the limits of integration, $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Since we want to express the integral in terms of the new variable, $u,$ we need to change the variable in the limits too.\n\nThe lower limit $x=1$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =1-1 \\\\ u & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile the upper limit $x=2$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =(2)-1 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is useful to use a table like the following when changing limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Solve the integral in the new variable $u,$ using the new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{6}-\\frac{1}{6}(0)^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when applying integration by substitution to definite integrals, there is no need to substitute the variable $x$ back in the final answer. We can simply evaluate it using the new variable $u.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186102029 1064 "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\\\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\\\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({ln}^{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186102029 1064 "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{