nates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(r, \\theta\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right).\n$$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ k & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-7\\cos\\theta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8aec18033a85129c44765d2670ebe9c931b0c0186c46b74f9b0ea0ae14028b76.png)" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186105613 1074 "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(r, \\theta\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ k & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-7\\cos\\theta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8aec18033a85129c44765d2670ebe9c931b0c0186c46b74f9b0ea0ae14028b76.png)" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186105652 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $f(1)=-\\dfrac15$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $g$ do not sum to $1{:}$\n$$\n\\dfrac25 + \\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10} \\neq 1\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n$$\n0 \\leq \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\dfrac12, \\dfrac15 \\leq 1\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac15 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186105652 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $f(1)=-\\dfrac15$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $g$ do not sum to $1{:}$\n$$\n\\dfrac25 + \\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10} \\neq 1\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n$$\n0 \\leq \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\dfrac12, \\dfrac15 \\leq 1\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac15 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186105670 1074 "We will first compute the number of ways of selecting $8$ chocolates. Then, we will compute the number of ways of selecting $4$ nougats. Finally, we will apply the multiplication principle to compute the total number of combinations that can be obtained.\n\n- The problem of selecting $8$ chocolates is analogous to placing $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories. We are choosing $r=8$ chocolates from $n=4$ categories. The number of ways to place $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is given by the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\nSo, the number of ways to choose $r=8$ chocolates from $n=4$ categories is\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((8 + 4 - 1)!)/(8!(4 - 1)!)|= \\left(\\frac{11}{8}\\right); = (11!)/(8! \\cdot (11 - 8)!); = \\frac{11!}{8! \\cdot 3!}; = 165.\n$$\n- The number of ways of choosing $4$ nougats from $5$ possible options without repetition is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)|= (5!)/(4! \\cdot (5 - 4)!); = \\frac{5!}{4! \\cdot 1!}; = 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the multiplication principle, we conclude that we can obtain\n\n$$\n165\\cdot 5 =825\n$$\n\ncombinations in total." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17