e] [17592186106493 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}f(x)dx$ is convergent\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{0}f(x)dx$ is divergent\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x)dx$ is divergent" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186106546 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186106546 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186107002 1064 "![](images/a9/a9a087086d2653ff95b039c1a05ea0dcf310df72243cfee248c8544f179adf37.png)\n\nConsider the parallelogram $ABCD$ above, where\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's find the measures of the other angles.\n\n- First, we know that opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent. Therefore, $m\\angle C = 70^\\circ$.![](images/3a/3a32408673bb4a42be19f48323476339ed7aaaf6c91243b29d8fc4fcb9f0818a.png)\n\n- Next, we know that consecutive angles in a parallelogram are supplementary. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m∠B|= 180^{∘} - m∠A; = 180^{∘} - 70^{∘}; = 110^{∘}.\n$$\nLet's add this to our diagram.![](images/f9/f92d9f2354b52d90074aaca802937468645749d09aa314f0fa915947ee3d4682.png)\n\n- Finally, we apply the fact that opposite angles are congruent to conclude that $m\\angle D = 110^\\circ$.![](images/d9/d91adf574a75ce2095129724e5eb241e0aebb6f0b3c8182c41972c36e5f876ef.png)" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186107002 1064 "![](images/a9/a9a087086d2653ff95b039c1a05ea0dcf310df72243cfee248c8544f179adf37.png)\n\nConsider the parallelogram $ABCD$ above, where\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's find the measures of the other angles.\n\n- First, we know that opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent. Therefore, $m\\angle C = 70^\\circ$.![](images/3a/3a32408673bb4a42be19f48323476339ed7aaaf6c91243b29d8fc4fcb9f0818a.png)\n\n- Next, we know that consecutive angles in a parallelogram are supplementary. Therefore,\n$m∠B|= 180^{∘} - m∠A; = 180^{∘} - 70^{∘}; = 110^{∘}$.\nLet's add this to our diagram.![](images/f9/f92d9f2354b52d90074aaca802937468645749d09aa314f0fa915947ee3d4682.png)\n\n- Finally, we apply the fact that opposite angles are congruent to conclude that $m\\angle D = 110^\\circ$.![](images/d9/d91adf574a75ce2095129724e5eb241e0aebb6f0b3c8182c41972c36e5f876ef.png)" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186107011 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of another important property of parallelograms.\n\n> *The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.*\n\nTo prove this theorem, consider the following parallelogram. Note that the diagonals are already constructed.\n\n![](images/a3/a3a387f45449207a466c6e32e9b17c15d63295ee01947f5abaea402802e372fa.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> *$VZ = XZ$ and $WZ = YZ$.*\n\nWe can complete the proof of this theorem using congruent triangles, as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We are *given* that $VWXY$ is a parallelogram.\n- **Step 2**: By the *definition of a parallelogram*, $\\overline{VW} \\parallel \\overline{XY}$.\n\n![](images/58/58256c1f636ba12e22b35721258595a1ebae88dee702a0866d8a7bb3c0c1b7ca.png)\n\n- **Step 3**: The transversal $\\overline{WY}$ crosses the parallel segments $\\overline{VW}$ and $\\overline{XY}$, forming the pair of alternate interior angles. Therefore, by the *alternate interior angles theorem*,\n$\\angle{VWZ}\\cong\\angle{XYZ}$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec43a425949749fc5872bbcc85b082f20b62c2581d35a03efca0dff4fb933904.png)\n\n- **Step 4**: Note that $\\angle{VZW}$ and $\\angle{XZY}$ are vertical angles. Therefore, by the *vertical angles theorem*,\n$\\angle{VZW}\\cong\\angle{XZY}$,\nas shown.\n\n![](images/b5/b574a606fb285425824c859578102609ea7a61813274207728dd022318ab0f59.png)\n\n- **Step 5**: Since $VWXY$ is a parallelogram, its opposite sides are congruent by the *properties of parallelograms.* Therefore,\n$\\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$,\nas shown.\n\n![](images/33/33b611374d96a948f088379430157095b6ff5d5a95d9a051f96951a34008aaef.png)\n\n- **Step 6**: From steps 3, 4, and 5, we have\n$\\angle{VWZ} \\cong \\angle{XYZ}, \\qquad \\angle{VZW} \\cong \\angle{XZY}, \\qquad \\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$.\nTherefore,\n$\\triangle{VWZ} \\cong \\triangle{XYZ}$\nby the *AAS criterion* for triangle congruence.\n- **Step 7**: Since corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent (*CPCTC*), we have\n$\\overline{VZ} \\cong \\overline{XZ}, \\qquad \\overline{WZ} \\cong \\overline{YZ}$.\n- **Step 8**: By the *definition of congruent segments*, we conclude that\n$$\n\\displaystyle VZ = XZ, \\qquad WZ = YZ,\n$$\nas required." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186107011 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of another important property of parallelograms.\n\n> *The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.*\n\nTo prove this theorem, consider the following parallelogram. Note that the diagonals are already constructed.\n\n![](images/a3/a3a387f45449207a466c6e32e9b17c15d63295ee01947f5abaea402802e372fa.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> *$VZ = XZ$ and $WZ = YZ$.*\n\nWe can complete the proof of this theorem using congruent triangles, as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We are *given* that $VWXY$ is a parallelogram.\n- **Step 2**: By the *definition of a parallelogram*, $\\overline{VW} \\parallel \\overline{XY}$.\n\n![](images/58/58256c1f636ba12e22b35721258595a1ebae88dee702a0866d8a7bb3c0c1b7ca.png)\n\n- **Step 3**: The transversal $\\overline{WY}$ crosses the parallel segments $\\overline{VW}$ and $\\overline{XY}$, forming the pair of alternate interior angles. Therefore, by the *alternate interior angles theorem*,\n$\\angle{VWZ}\\cong\\angle{XYZ}$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec43a425949749fc5872bbcc85b082f20b62c2581d35a03efca0dff4fb933904.png)\n\n- **Step 4**: Note that $\\angle{VZW}$ and $\\angle{XZY}$ are vertical angles. Therefore, by the *vertical angles theorem*,\n$\\angle{VZW}\\cong\\angle{XZY}$,\nas shown.\n\n![](images/b5/b574a606fb285425824c859578102609ea7a61813274207728dd022318ab0f59.png)\n\n- **Step 5**: Since $VWXY$ is a parallelogram, its opposite sides are congruent by the *properties of parallelograms.* Therefore,\n$\\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$,\nas shown.\n\n![](images/33/33b611374d96a948f088379430157095b6ff5d5a95d9a051f96951a34008aaef.png)\n\n- **Step 6**: From steps 3, 4, and 5, we have\n$\\angle{VWZ} \\cong \\angle{XYZ}, \\qquad \\angle{VZW} \\cong \\angle{XZY}, \\qquad \\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$.\nTherefore,\n$\\triangle{VWZ} \\cong \\triangle{XYZ}$\nby the *AAS criterion* for triangle congruence.\n- **Step 7**: Since corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent (*CPCTC*), we have\n$\\overline{VZ} \\cong \\overline{XZ}, \\qquad \\overline{WZ} \\cong \\overline{YZ}$.\n- **Step 8**: By the *definition of congruent segments*, we conclude that\n$VZ = XZ, \\qquad WZ = YZ$,\nas required." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186107038 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2.\n$$ To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2,$ which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty.$ On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty.\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186107038 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2.$ To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2,$ which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty.$ On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty.\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186107110 1064 "Here, we derive the three useful triangle formulas that we have used throughout the lesson.\n\n- For an equilateral triangle with side length $a$, the area $A$ is given by the formula\n$A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2$.\nTo see why this is true, consider the equilateral triangle below.![](images/85/85b54af73ee6951c83aa675e7e34c6403c91d3f8d11ca198c1a4f91510e8d976.png)\nFor this triangle, we see that we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle base|= a,; height|= a\\sin 60^{∘} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a.\n$$\nSubstituting this into the formula for the area of a triangle, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot a \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}a = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2.\n$$\n\n![](images/20/20b7814be68e26f3e4b0426f6726d571a2dc0c112705dc6cad178bf0042d23a3.png)\n\n- For an isosceles right triangle with leg $b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the area $A$ of the triangle is\n$A = \\dfrac12 b^2$.\nThis is a straightforward application of the formula for the area of a triangle. Clearly, we have $\\textrm{base} = \\textrm{height} = b$, and therefore, the formula follows immediately.We also have the formula\n$A = \\dfrac14 c^2$.\nTo see why this is true, recall that, for an isosceles right triangle, the base $b$ and hypotenuse $c$ are related by the equation $c = \\sqrt 2 b$. Now, substituting for $b$ into the formula $A=\\dfrac12 b^2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\dfrac12\\left(\\dfrac{c}{\\sqrt 2}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac14 c^2.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186107110 1064 "Here, we derive the three useful triangle formulas that we have used throughout the lesson.\n\n- For an equilateral triangle with side length $a$, the area $A$ is given by the formula\n$A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2$.\nTo see why this is true, consider the equilateral triangle below.![](images/85/85b54af73ee6951c83aa675e7e34c6403c91d3f8d11ca198c1a4f91510e8d976.png)\nFor this triangle, we see that we have\n$base|= a,; height|= a\\sin 60^{∘} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}a$.\nSubstituting this into the formula for the area of a triangle, we get\n$A = \\dfrac 1 2 \\cdot a \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}a = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}a^2$.\n\n![](images/20/20b7814be68e26f3e4b0426f6726d571a2dc0c112705dc6cad178bf0042d23a3.png)\n\n- For an isosceles right triangle with leg $b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the area $A$ of the triangle is\n$A = \\dfrac12 b^2$.\nThis is a straightforward application of the formula for the area of a triangle. Clearly, we have $\\textrm{base} = \\textrm{height} = b$, and therefore, the formula follows immediately.We also have the formula\n$A = \\dfrac14 c^2$.\nTo see why this is true, recall that, for an isosceles right triangle, the base $b$ and hypotenuse $c$ are related by the equation $c = \\sqrt 2 b$. Now, substituting for $b$ into the formula $A=\\dfrac12 b^2$, we get\n$A = \\dfrac12\\left(\\dfrac{c}{\\sqrt 2}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac14 c^2$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186107113 1064 "We've been using the fact that the number of ways to place $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where this formula comes from, it's important to understand one key insight:\n\n> Placing $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is the same as placing $r$ identical objects around $n-1$ dividers.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to place $r=4$ stars stickers into $n=3$ boxes. Then this is the same as placing the stars around $3-1=2$ dividers. Several arrangements are as follows. Each divider is denoted with a $\"\\mid\"$.\n\n- The first box has $2$ stars, the second box has $1$ star, and the third box has $1$ star:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad}_{1\\textrm{st box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{2\\textrm{nd box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{3\\textrm{rd box}}$\n- The first box has $1$ star, the second box has $3$ stars, and the third box has no stars:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{1\\textrm{st box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad}_{2\\textrm{nd box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\phantom{\\star} \\quad}_{3\\textrm{rd box}}$\n- The first box has no stars, the second box has no stars, and the third box has all $4$ stars:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\phantom{\\star} \\quad}_{1\\textrm{st box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\phantom{\\star} \\quad}_{2\\textrm{nd box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad}_{3\\textrm{rd box}}$\n\nTo count the number of ways to place $4$ stars around $2$ dividers, notice that there are\n\n$$\n4+2=6\n$$\n\nspots that can be filled with either a star or a divider. To illustrate, let's label those $6$ spots in each of the above arrangements.\n\n- The first box has $2$ stars, the second box has $1$ star, and the third box has $1$ star:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}⋆ & ⋆ & ∣ & ⋆ & ∣ & ⋆ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- The first box has $1$ star, the second box has $3$ stars, and the third box has no stars:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}⋆ & ∣ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ∣ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- The first box has no stars, the second box has no stars, and the third box has all $4$ stars:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}∣ & ∣ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ⋆ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe number of ways to place $4$ stars around $2$ dividers is equal to the number of ways to choose $4$ of the $6$ spots (in which to place stars):\n\n$$\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\dfrac{6!}{4! \\, 2!} = 15\n$$\n\nMore generally, the number of ways to place $r$ stars around $n-1$ dividers is equal to the number of ways to choose $r$ of the $r+n-1$ spots (in which to place stars):\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The dividers are also known as \"bars,\" so this method of deriving the formula is often referred to as the \"stars and bars\" method." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186107113 1064 "We've been using the fact that the number of ways to place $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where this formula comes from, it's important to understand one key insight:\n\n> Placing $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is the same as placing $r$ identical objects around $n-1$ dividers.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to place $r=4$ stars stickers into $n=3$ boxes. Then this is the same as placing the stars around $3-1=2$ dividers. Several arrangements are as follows. Each divider is denoted with a $\"\\mid\"$.\n\n- The first box has $2$ stars, the second box has $1$ star, and the third box has $1$ star:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad}_{1\\textrm{st box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{2\\textrm{nd box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{3\\textrm{rd box}}$\n- The first box has $1$ star, the second box has $3$ stars, and the third box has no stars:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\