" method." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186107133 1064 "In general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function for a random variable $X$ on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to the conditions above.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$ states that any possible outcome for $X$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1\n$$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ must add up to $1.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186107133 1064 "In general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function for a random variable $X$ on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to the conditions above.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$ states that any possible outcome for $X$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ must add up to $1.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186107141 1064 "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1\n$$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6.$ In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected. \n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3,$ we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6],$ we must have $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186107141 1064 "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6.$ In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected. \n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3,$ we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6],$ we must have $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186110298 1071 "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{y}{2}\\right)^3 \\left(\\dfrac{2y}{3}\\right)^2.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186110298 1071 "Simplify $\\left(\\dfrac{y}{2}\\right)^3 \\left(\\dfrac{2y}{3}\\right)^2.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186110314 1071 "Plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x.\n$$" 131