 \\\\\n&= x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 2x^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x^{2} - 4) + 2(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 - 8\n$$\n\nare $x = \\pm{2}$, which are all simple roots.\n\nNext, we graph the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/b6/b65299eb9e1c35485c1af5a1f30c8c6ac14e03e7566a5f562e4e420ccb417a60.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0$, we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (2,\\infty)$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186110527 1074 "To solve this inequality, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}P(x) & > 2x^{2} \\\\ x^{4} - 8 & > 2x^{2} \\\\ x^{4} - 2x^{2} - 8 & > 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo solve the inequality $P(x) \\gt 2x^2$, we solve the equivalent problem $f(x) \\gt 0$, where\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 - 8\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nWe factor the polynomial $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{4} - 2x^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 2x^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x^{2} - 4) + 2(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 - 8\n$$\n\nare $x = \\pm{2}$, which are all simple roots.\n\nNext, we graph the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/b6/b65299eb9e1c35485c1af5a1f30c8c6ac14e03e7566a5f562e4e420ccb417a60.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0$, we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (2,\\infty)$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186110544 1074 "For each row/column in a joint frequency table:\n\n- the sum of the joint frequencies equals the marginal frequenc