ac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=15$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=91,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 91≈6.1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186115973 1071 "Compute the variance $σ^{2}$ of a data set $x_{i}$ consisting of $12$ data points, where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i} = 36, ∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i}^{2} = 132.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186115973 1071 "Compute the variance $σ^{2}$ of a data set $x_{i}$ consisting of $12$ data points, where\n\n$∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i} = 36, ∑_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i}^{2} = 132$." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186115977 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 y - 7 x = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle - x + 2 y = - 1\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186115977 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 y - 7 x = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle - x + 2 y = - 1\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116422 1064 "Consider a body of mass $m$ falling vertically, influenced only by\n\n- gravity $mg$, and\n- air resistance (drag) $D$.\n\nAssume that both $m$ and $g$ are constant, and choose the downward direction as the positive direction.\n\n![](images/9e/9ecacfcd964cb91f1cc35c4b9abe8e0969b85c02703eaf34e26612a9a624fdcc.png)\n\nThere are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity is the weight, which equals $mg$.\n- The drag force is $D \\leq 0$, since it acts upward (in the negative direction).\n\nHence, the net force on the body is $F = mg + D.$\n\nIf drag is negligible, then $D=0$, so the net force becomes $F=mg.$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the body, we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle F & \\displaystyle  = m g \\\\ \\displaystyle m a & \\displaystyle  = m g \\\\ \\displaystyle a & \\displaystyle  = g .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, since acceleration is the derivative of velocity, $a=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}$, we obtain $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116422 1064 "Consider a body of mass $m$ falling vertically, influenced only by\n\n- gravity $mg$, and\n- air resistance (drag) $D$.\n\nAssume that both $m$ and $g$ are constant, and choose the downward direction as the positive direction.\n\n![](images/9e/9ecacfcd964cb91f1cc35c4b9abe8e0969b85c02703eaf34e26612a9a624fdcc.png)\n\nThere are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity is the weight, which equals $mg$.\n- The drag force is $D \\leq 0$, since it acts upward (in the negative direction).\n\nHence, the net force on the body is $F = mg + D.$\n\nIf drag is negligible, then $D=0$, so the net force becomes $F=mg.$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma$, where $a$ is the acceleration of the body, we get $\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle F & \\displaystyle  = m g \\\\ \\displaystyle m a & \\displaystyle  = m g \\\\ \\displaystyle a & \\displaystyle  = g .\\end{array}$\n\nFinally, since acceleration is the derivative of velocity, $a=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}$, we obtain $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116427 1064 "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.\n$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, \n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0.\n$$\n\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116427 1064 "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.\n$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, \n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0.\n$$\n\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116431 1064 "For any power function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nwe can compute the limit $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} x^n\n$$ by substituting $x=a$ directly into the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} x^n = a^n \\,\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2,\n$$ we can substitute $x=3$ directly into the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2 = 3^2 = 9 \\,\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result by graphing the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nand checking the left and right-sided limits.\n\n![](images/69/69cea7ab1cfb0c0a2201dd857b12eafa9ecf0cf4824abb5d4ca0234e40f3af5f.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we compute the left and right-sided limits:\n\n- Left-sided limit: $$\n\\displaystyle \\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3^-} x^2 = 9\n$$\n- Right-sided limit: $$\n\\displaystyle \\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3^+} x^2 = 9\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits both evaluate to $9$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2 = 9 \\,\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116431 1064 "For any power function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nwe can compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} x^n$ by substituting $x=a$ directly into the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} x^n = a^n \\,\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2$, we can substitute $x=3$ directly into the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2 = 3^2 = 9 \\,\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result by graphing the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nand checking the left and right-sided limits.\n\n![](images/69/69cea7ab1cfb0c0a2201dd857b12eafa9ecf0cf4824abb5d4ca0234e40f3af5f.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we compute the left and right-sided limits:\n\n- Left-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3^-} x^2 = 9$\n- Right-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3^+} x^2 = 9$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits both evaluate to $9$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 3} x^2 = 9 \\,\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116432 1064 "For any constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=c\n$$\n\nthe limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} c$ will match the value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} c=c \\,\n$$\n\nFor example, the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} 1$ is just the value of the constant, $1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} 1 = 1\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result by graphing the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1\n$$\n\n![](images/a2/a2c17e59dca9161db435fc4b3edf78a22fed7e6ee94219ac29b85627116791ac.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we compute the left and right-sided limits:\n\n- Left-sided limit: $$\n\\displaystyle \\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} 1 = 1\n$$\n- Right-sided limit: $$\n\\displaystyle \\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} 1 = 1\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits both evaluate to $1$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} 1 = 1 \\,\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116432 1064 "For any constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=c\n$$\n\nthe limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} c$ will match the value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} c=c \\,\n$$\n\nFor example, the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} 1$ is just the value of the constant, $1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} 1 = 1\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result by graphing the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1\n$$\n\n![](images/a2/a2c17e59dca9161db435fc4b3edf78a22fed7e6ee94219ac29b85627116791ac.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we compute the left and right-sided limits:\n\n- Left-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} 1 = 1$\n- Right-sided limit: $\\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} 1 = 1$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits both evaluate to $1$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} 1 = 1 \\,\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116433 1064 "The **constant rule for limits** states that if $c$ is a constant, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}c\\cdot f(x) = c\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=c\\cdot L\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}5x^3,\n$$ we just factor the constant $5$ out of the limit and then substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}{\\color{blue}5}x^3 &= {\\color{blue}5} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}x^3 \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}5}\\cdot 2^3 \\\\[5pt] &= 40 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, consider the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,1} cf(x) = 10}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a real constant. Let's find the value of $c$.\n\n![](images/de/dea6bb963e83928f463ac2b6d09775b0c11ac0ca7c8e3e567c4b51da154c86f9.png)\n\nFrom the figure above, we get that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\, 1} f(x)} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)cf(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot 5 &= 10 \\\\\nc &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116433 1064 "The **constant rule for limits** states that if $c$ is a constant, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}c\\cdot f(x) = c\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=c\\cdot L\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}5x^3$, we just factor the constant $5$ out of the limit and then substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}{\\color{blue}5}x^3 &= {\\color{blue}5} \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}x^3 \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}5}\\cdot 2^3 \\\\[5pt] &= 40 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, consider the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,1} cf(x) = 10}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a real constant. Let's find the value of $c$.\n\n![](images/de/dea6bb963e83928f463ac2b6d09775b0c11ac0ca7c8e3e567c4b51da154c86f9.png)\n\nFrom the figure above, we get that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\, 1} f(x)} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)cf(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot 5 &= 10 \\\\\nc &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116435 1064 "Consider the graph of $y=e^x$ below. We will use this graph to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} e^x \\, .\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d83670c0d8b06b6412c8ca22a73a91ca0c2e05290eb0dbe34418cdf8e8f242c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $y$ approaches $e^1.$ So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} e^x = e^1 = e.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the function at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, and this is true for *any* point, not just $x=1.$ If $c$ is any real number, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} e^x = e^c.\n$$\n\nIn fact, *all* exponential functions have this property. For $a \\gt 0$ and any real number $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} a^x = a^c.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116435 1064 "Consider the graph of $y=e^x$ below. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} e^x \\, .$\n\n![](images/9d/9d83670c0d8b06b6412c8ca22a73a91ca0c2e05290eb0dbe34418cdf8e8f242c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $y$ approaches $e^1.$ So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} e^x = e^1 = e.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the function at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, and this is true for *any* point, not just $x=1.$ If $c$ is any real number, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} e^x = e^c.\n$$\n\nIn fact, *all* exponential functions have this property. For $a \\gt 0$ and any real number $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} a^x = a^c.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116437 1064 "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).\n$$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116437 1064 "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,\\underbrace{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116456 1064 "The **secant** is the reciprocal of the cosine:\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nTo plot the graph of $y=\\sec x,$ with $x$ in radians, we create a table with some known values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-\\frac\\pi 3$ | $-\\frac\\pi 4$ | $-\\frac\\pi 6$ | $0$ | $\\frac\\pi 6$ | $\\frac\\pi 4$ | $\\frac\\pi 3$ | $\\frac\\pi 2$ | $\\frac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\pi$ | $\\frac{5\\pi} 4$ | $\\frac{3\\pi} 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\cos x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $\\sec x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt 2$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $\\sqrt 2$ | $2$ | undef. | $-\\sqrt 2$ | $-1$ | $-\\sqrt 2$ | undef. |\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve:\n\n![](images/50/50cc78897f9962e84d6c593bf6be1f1cb426fb3988008d3c03b89d3f1aae16fe.png)\n\nSince the period of $\\cos x$ is $2\\pi,$ the period of $\\sec x$ is also $2\\pi.$ Since $\\cos x$ is an even function, the function $\\sec x$ is even. So we extend the plot with this periodicity property.\n\n![](images/ca/cada30731aa08377fc3bb1838455ced89d8bb7b16b644b6d9bafc22916553e27.png)\n\nSome key characteristics about $y=\\sec x$ are:\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty)$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots\n$$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, +\\infty).$\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi.$\n\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116456 1064 "The **secant** is the reciprocal of the cosine:\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nTo plot the graph of $y=\\sec x,$ with $x$ in radians, we create a table with some known values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-\\frac\\pi 3$ | $-\\frac\\pi 4$ | $-\\frac\\pi 6$ | $0$ | $\\frac\\pi 6$ | $\\frac\\pi 4$ | $\\frac\\pi 3$ | $\\frac\\pi 2$ | $\\frac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\pi$ | $\\frac{5\\pi} 4$ | $\\frac{3\\pi} 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\cos x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ |\n| $\\sec x$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt 2$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $\\sqrt 2$ | $2$ | undef. | $-\\sqrt 2$ | $-1$ | $-\\sqrt 2$ | undef. |\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve:\n\n![](images/50/50cc78897f9962e84d6c593bf6be1f1cb426fb3988008d3c03b89d3f1aae16fe.png)\n\nSince the period of $\\cos x$ is $2\\pi,$ the period of $\\sec x$ is also $2\\pi.$ Since $\\cos x$ is an even function, the function $\\sec x$ is even. So we extend the plot with this periodicity property.\n\n![](images/ca/cada30731aa08377fc3bb1838455ced89d8bb7b16b644b6d9bafc22916553e27.png)\n\nSome key characteristics about $y=\\sec x$ are:\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty)$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, +\\infty).$\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi.$\n\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots.$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 false] [17592186116464 1064 "We can solve an equation involving secant over any desired domain. For example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x = 2, \\qquad -2\\pi < x \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe can proceed as usual. Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${\\color{red}x_1}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](images/31/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution $\\color{blue}x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x_2} = 2\\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nHowever, we're not done yet. The domain $-2\\pi < x \\leq 2\\pi$ is larger than one period, so there are more than two solutions.\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the desired domain, we need to find angles that are coterminal with ${\\color{red}x_1}$ or ${\\color{blue}x_2}.$ So, we subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all possible solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_3 & = {\\color{red}x_1} -2\\pi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}- 2\\pi= -\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3},\\\\\nx_4 &= {\\color{blue}x_2} -2\\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}- 2\\pi= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding or subtracting any more multiples of $2\\pi$ would result in solutions outside the desired domain. So, we have found all the solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are  $$\n\\displaystyle x = \\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pm \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139539081 17592186576411 true] [17592186116464 1064 "We can solve an equation involving secant over any desired domain. For example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x = 2, \\qquad -2\\pi < x \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe can proceed as usual. Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${\\color{red}x_1}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](images/31/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a771